      "versionNonce": 1884146060,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1792803782402,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Workspace: \n- Think of it as a main organisaztion for smaller teams\n- Small teams ususally have small set of products\n- Each workspace will divide the products into teams \n(backend of the applicaion, frontend of the application, \nandriod app team, ios app team, internal tooling team)\n- The workspace as a whole can access the core resources of any teams.\n- Core resources includes docs, decisions, glossary, maps. \n- Linear does not have docs as a seperate feature. We do\n- But we want wheelhouse to be a fast and efficient memory for AI agents \nso when they work on issue they get the best available context on the task.\n- This good context architecture needs to be researched and refined. (we were looking into supermemory for what it does)\n\nOnboarding and Usecase (Most Important)\n- If some dev of team wants to use a project management tool then wheelhouse should clearly make a proposition that\nsolves the core problems smaller and solo devs are facing in the AI heavy agentic workflow (espsecially when everyone is vibecoding)\n\nAgentic Workflows\n- The core application is similar to linear. \n- Projects, Teams, Issues, Docs, Code Repository, Diffs\n- The differentator is leaning more on AI to speed up the flow\n- While the long plan is to use dedicated Wheelhouse agents to do certain parts\n- Right now we are allowing teams to make the most of their AI subscriptions and their own\nmachines by letting them install a daemon \n- The daemon can detect the supported AI subscriptions (Claude, Ollama, Codex, Gemini)\n- The daemon then connects to wheelhouse allowing the team-memeber to see their devices and the agents\non that device ready to use.\n- The issues then that are created can be run on those devices by selecting the models. (a worktree is generated\nvia the github and then the issue is worked on and a PR is raised.\n- The progress and status can be tracked in wheelhouse.\n- There is also some thought of giving custom AI agents specialized for a task and making them available in a team\n- These agents are a persona via prompt that a team member can use to work on a task\n- There is a discussion on how this fits with the already existing run on device and a architecure design is needed.\n\nCI/CD platform\n- There is some discussions on how can wheelhouse help in managing visibility into the deployment environments\n- Also on how things can be controlled form the wheelhouse for deployments.\n- There is thought of primarily supporting VPS based deployments, AWS (infra)\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Workspace: \n- Think of it as a main organisaztion for smaller teams\n- Small teams ususally have small set of products\n- Each workspace will divide the products into teams \n(backend of the applicaion, frontend of the application, \nandriod app team, ios app team, internal tooling team)\n- The workspace as a whole can access the core resources of any teams.\n- Core resources includes docs, decisions, glossary, maps. \n- Linear does not have docs as a seperate feature. We do\n- But we want wheelhouse to be a fast and efficient memory for AI agents \nso when they work on issue they get the best available context on the task.\n- This good context architecture needs to be researched and refined. (we were looking into supermemory for what it does)\n\nOnboarding and Usecase (Most Important)\n- If some dev of team wants to use a project management tool then wheelhouse should clearly make a proposition that\nsolves the core problems smaller and solo devs are facing in the AI heavy agentic workflow (espsecially when everyone is vibecoding)\n\nAgentic Workflows\n- The core application is similar to linear. \n- Projects, Teams, Issues, Docs, Code Repository, Diffs\n- The differentator is leaning more on AI to speed up the flow\n- While the long plan is to use dedicated Wheelhouse agents to do certain parts\n- Right now we are allowing teams to make the most of their AI subscriptions and their own\nmachines by letting them install a daemon \n- The daemon can detect the supported AI subscriptions (Claude, Ollama, Codex, Gemini)\n- The daemon then connects to wheelhouse allowing the team-memeber to see their devices and the agents\non that device ready to use.\n- The issues then that are created can be run on those devices by selecting the models. (a worktree is generated\nvia the github and then the issue is worked on and a PR is raised.\n- The progress and status can be tracked in wheelhouse.\n- There is also some thought of giving custom AI agents specialized for a task and making them available in a team\n- These agents are a persona via prompt that a team member can use to work on a task\n- There is a discussion on how this fits with the already existing run on device and a architecure design is needed.\n\nCI/CD platform\n- There is some discussions on how can wheelhouse help in managing visibility into the deployment environments\n- Also on how things can be controlled form the wheelhouse for deployments.\n- There is thought of primarily supporting VPS based deployments, AWS (infra)\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
